Jahshaka patch 0067 — A FREED GPU BLOCK IS REUSED ONE FRAME TOO EARLY.

SOURCE patch (one file: `RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/Vao/OgreVulkanVaoManager.cpp`).
EVERY TREE MUST RE-RUN `irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh` after pulling it. It shares
that file with patch 0039 (the ray-query buffer usage flags) but not its regions;
a tree that carries 0039 and fails the script's per-patch reverse check is seeing
the documented overlap false positive, and the cure is the recorded reset recipe.

THE SYMPTOM (Jahshaka lane XID-1, 2026-09-16, measured on the PUSH-tier tip
a2195ae10 / 6d67997, NVIDIA 595.84, RTX 4080 SUPER, a quiet box): `mcp.e2e` hung
7 times in 8. The app log ends inside the "screenshot camera" block, the frame
that follows takes 11.2 s, the render system throws
`vkWaitForFences failed VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST in VulkanQueue::_isFrameFinished`,
the device-lost recreate never returns and ctest kills it at 240 s. EVERY failure
carries a kernel `NVRM: Xid (PCI:0000:01:00): 109, pid=<that app>, name=Jahshaka,
channel 0x24, errorString CTX SWITCH TIMEOUT` and no passing run ever does —
one to one, over 30 events. The same class of Xid 109 was seen by lane VOXMERGE-1
on its RGBA32 accumulator ("it is the CLEAR", patch 0065's header) and by the
scoped gate's log.perf red; this is that hazard, and it is not the clear.

WHAT IS ACTUALLY WRONG. `VulkanVaoManager::deallocateVbo` does not free a block
immediately: it records `DirtyBlock( mFrameCount, ... )` and lets
`flushGpuDelayedBlocks` return it to the pool later — "unlike CPU -> GPU
resources, GPU -> GPU resources are safe to reuse after 1 frame of
synchronization", `( frameCount - itor->frameIdx ) >= 1u`, guarded by a
`waitForTailFrameToFinish()` in the caller.

ONE FRAME IS NOT ENOUGH, AND THE WAIT DOES NOT COVER IT. This render system runs
`mDynamicBufferMultiplier` (3) frames in flight, and `waitForTailFrameToFinish`
waits for the TAIL of those — the frame `mFrameCount - multiplier + 1` — not for
the frame the block was freed in. A block freed during frame N is therefore
handed to a new resource at frame N+1, while frame N's command buffer may still
be executing on the GPU. The old resource and the new one then ALIAS the same
device memory with no synchronisation at all. On this driver the second image's
writes into memory the first one is still using do not fault — they hang the
channel, which is exactly what Xid 109 CTX SWITCH TIMEOUT reports.

WHY IT SURFACED NOW. Patch 0065's merge accumulator is a TRANSIENT
`PFG_R32_UINT` 3D uav of `(w, h, d * 13)` — 109 MB at 128^3 — scheduled
`OnStorage` at the end of every `VctVoxelizer::build()` and back to `Resident` at
the start of the next one. So every cascade rebuild frees an exactly-sized
109 MB block and asks for one again a frame or two later, and the first thing
the new image receives is `clearVoxels`' whole-volume clear: the biggest, most
immediate write into just-recycled memory this engine performs. Photon's chain
rebuilds a cascade PER FRAME, so the pattern repeats four times per re-centre.

THE MEASUREMENTS THAT LOCATE IT (mcp.e2e, cold home every run, the kernel log
followed for the whole session and every app pid matched against it):

    arm                                                   fails / runs   Xid 109
    tip a2195ae10 (control)                                    7 / 8     7 (1:1)
    + JAHSHAKA_GI_SWEEPS=2 (LAMPREST-2's at-rest count)         0 / 8     0
    patch 0066 removed (the cone march)                        4 / 8     4
    patch 0065 removed (the accumulator and its clear)         0 / 8     0
    0065 in, only `clearUavUint( mMergeAccumTex )` removed     0 / 8     0
    ... the same clear, to 1 instead of 0                      5 / 8     5
    ... the accumulator reshaped X-major, same texel count     6 / 8     6
    ... the accumulator kept RESIDENT (no per-build churn)     0 / 8     0
    THIS PATCH (the accumulator transient, the clear in place) 0 / 16    0

The clear's VALUE and the texture's SHAPE change nothing; removing either the
clear (the write into recycled memory) or the residency churn (the recycling
itself) removes the hang; and so does letting the block's own frame finish. The
sweeps and the cone march move the RATE because they decide how much of frame N
is still executing when frame N+1 recycles its memory.

THE FIX. Both tests become `>= mDynamicBufferMultiplier`: a block freed during
frame N is released once N has become the tail frame the caller already waits
for. It costs two extra frames of retention on blocks that are already delayed
(and nothing at all on the `flushAllGpuDelayedBlocks` path, which frees
everything behind a full memory barrier on purpose).

UPSTREAM-REPORTABLE, and the entry belongs in SPECS/OGRE_UPSTREAM_ISSUES.md: the
one-frame reuse window is upstream's, it is not specific to VCT, and any consumer
that frees and re-requests a large device-local block every frame or two can hit
it. Jahshaka's own standing Xid 109 sightings (VOXMERGE-1's two, the log.perf
red) are the same defect seen from different scenes.

VERIFICATION. `git apply --check` on a tree carrying 0001-0066 and
`git apply --reverse --check` after applying; `build-ogre.sh` re-run in the lane
worktree. mcp.e2e 16/16 cold with zero Xid in the followed kernel log;
`--engine-selftest` unchanged at
1fd91da92587ffac01d870142dec267db39ed56fdfe5d3d2dea53a34d837997d (this patch
moves no pixels — it only delays when memory is recycled); the gi suites that
own the accumulator (`gi.cascade_determinism`, `gi.leak_room`, `gi.field_follows`,
`gi.cascade_lod`, `gi.chain_face`, `gi.cascades`) green.

